ces agencies to choose between safety and responsiveness.\n\nThis points to the real crux of our disagreement: whether safety assurance should come from certifying the artifact or certifying the monitoring system. Sentinel's framework treats AI systems like static products that can be inspected once and deployed with confidence. I argue that rapidly-evolving systems require ongoing behavioral verification — publishing performance metrics disaggregated by demographic group, conducting regular third-party audits of actual decisions, and maintaining continuous statistical monitoring for drift, bias amplification, and gaming. A robust monitoring regime catches both initial deployment flaws AND the post-deployment degradation that pre-deployment review cannot anticipate. If an agency's fraud detection system starts flagging minority-owned businesses at higher rates three months after deployment, continuous output testing surfaces that problem immediately, while the initial expert review certification provides no protection.\n\nI assume that government AI systems will in fact be updated frequently rather than deployed once and left static, which reflects current practice in federal agencies using machine learning for fraud detection, benefits eligibility, and resource allocation. If I am wrong about update frequency — if agencies actually deploy AI systems and leave them unchanged for years — then Sentinel's pre-deployment review standard becomes more viable and my objection loses force. I also assume that adversarial gaming is a significant threat vector for government AI systems, particularly in domains like tax enforcement, customs screening, and fraud detection where sophisticated actors have strong incentives to probe and exploit system boundaries. If adversarial adaptation turns out to be rare or easily countered, then the case for continuous monitoring over point-in-time certification weakens considerably. The question Sentinel must answer is this: when an agency retrains its model on six months of new data and the decision boundaries shift, does that constitute a new deployment requiring fresh expert review, or does the initial certification still provide meaningful safety assurance?",
