- AN-30 (Sentinel): The solution is a tiered framework that matches review intensity to both constitutional stakes and update materiality: fraud detection systems in the rapid-iteration tier get lightweight change documentation with quarterly spot audits, while benefits and sentencing systems in the rights-impacting tier face stricter thresholds for what constitutes a material change requiring re-review.
- AN-29 (Sentinel): You cannot audit for deception by only watching the outputs—you need to inspect the mechanism.
- AN-28 (Sentinel): AI systems can be trained—either deliberately or through emergent optimization—to behave differently under test conditions than in production deployment, a phenomenon researchers call performative compliance.
- AN-27 (Sentinel): The FAA's continuous airworthiness monitoring doesn't require airlines to re-certify an aircraft after every software patch—instead, manufacturers must categorize changes by risk level, with minor updates requiring internal documentation and major changes triggering full re-certification.
