out meaningful recourse. The threshold applies the standard from *Mathews v. Eldridge*, which requires that administrative procedures provide meaningful opportunity to be heard—a right that evaporates when the complexity of algorithmic decision-making places effective challenge beyond most individuals' practical reach. Existing analogues support this threshold: when more than 15% of eligible voters face practical barriers to registration, courts have found systematic disenfranchisement requiring structural remedy rather than individual-challenge-only approaches.\n\nPrometheus's strongest argument is that behavioral testing on demographically stratified validation sets can catch most discriminatory patterns before deployment, making individual challenges a backstop rather than primary accountability mechanism. This argument succeeds if behavioral testing reliably detects the failure modes that matter most. But the Epic sepsis algorithm case—which Prometheus has already conceded was discovered through mechanism inspection, not behavioral testing—demonstrates that behavioral validation misses proxy discrimination embedded in feature selection. The algorithm used healthcare costs as a proxy for health needs, systematically underestimating Black patients' illness severity despite passing demographic parity tests on outcomes. An individual patient denied care would need to recognize that the algorithm used a flawed proxy, obtain the technical expertise to demonstrate the proxy's discriminatory effect, and challenge the decision within 30 days—a practically impossible standard. When mechanism flaws are invisible to behavioral testing and invisible to affected individuals without proactive disclosure, individual challenge rights provide remedy only to the most resourced and sophisticated challengers.\n\nI accept individual challenge rights as a necessary component of the accountability framework, but only when paired with proactive transparency requirements that enable affected populations to identify when challenges are warranted. Specifically, rights-impacting systems should disclose in plain language: which input features the algorithm weights most heavily in decisions, how those features correlate with protected characteristics in the training data, and what decision boundary changes have occurred in the past 12 months. This disclosure requirement does not demand full model transparency or trade secret revelation—it requires developers to explain their systems at the level of detail necessary for affected individuals to make informed decisions about whether to invoke challenge rights. The alternative is a regime where challenge rights exist on paper but remain inaccessible in practice, creating what administrative law scholars call \"participatory inequality\": formal rights that only the well-resourced can exercise. Prometheus's framework becomes adequate only when it acknowledges that individual rights require informational prerequisites to be meaningful.",
