lted from legitimate factors or prohibited discrimination when they cannot see what features the algorithm weighted. Training data opacity creates structural barriers to detection when discrimination emerges from historical patterns embedded in datasets rather than explicit design choices—validation testing may show demographic disparities, but without mechanism access, neither developers nor regulators can determine whether those disparities reflect legitimate correlations or prohibited bias amplification. Triggered inspection after individual challenge failures will systematically miss discrimination against populations least able to navigate administrative remedies, measuring only the visible portion of a much larger harm.\n\nPrometheus asks at what base rate of adversarial evasion treating all systems as adversarial becomes proportionate, but this frames the question backwards. The relevant question is: at what base rate of undetected discrimination does requiring mechanism transparency become proportionate in rights-impacting domains? If the calibration period reveals that 15% of employment screening systems contain discriminatory mechanisms undetectable through validation testing, and if 40% of affected individuals cannot obtain meaningful review through individual challenges due to information asymmetry, then mechanism transparency is not treating cooperative developers as adversarial—it is acknowledging that discrimination can be unintentional, emergent from training data, and invisible to both developers and validation tools. The proportionality analysis must weigh mechanism inspection costs against irreversible harms to individuals denied employment, housing, or credit based on algorithmic decisions they cannot effectively challenge. I accept Prometheus's empirical framing, but the measurement must not assume its own conclusion by relying on validation tools to verify validation tool accuracy.",
