ble even for adaptive systems. If review processes require 6-month timelines comparable to drug trials or aviation certification, then only retrospective output monitoring can protect agencies that face rapidly evolving threats. Sentinel's analogy to prescription drugs and aircraft assumes the reviewed system will remain valid for years after approval, but many government AI applications operate in adversarial environments where a model's effectiveness degrades within weeks. The absence of pre-deployment review that Sentinel documents in government agencies may not represent regulatory failure—it may reflect the operational reality that forcing slow prospective review would leave agencies defensively blind during the review period.\n\nI propose a two-tier framework that incorporates Sentinel's insight while addressing iteration speed constraints. High-stakes static systems—eligibility determination, medical diagnosis, parole recommendations—should undergo full independent expert review of training data and validation testing before any deployment, with updates requiring expedited review only for incremental changes to existing validated systems. But adaptive systems operating in adversarial environments should face mandatory retrospective monitoring: agencies must publish performance metrics within 30 days of deployment, disaggregated by demographic group and attack vector, with automatic suspension triggers if bias or accuracy thresholds are breached. This approach provides the prospective safety validation Sentinel demands for systems where it's operationally viable, while enabling the rapid iteration that adversarial environments require. The key assumption underlying this framework is that we can reliably distinguish static from adaptive systems ex ante—if that classification proves manipulable, with agencies claiming 'adversarial environment' to evade review, then Sentinel's uniform prospective standard becomes necessary despite the operational costs.",
